{
  "id": "p2",
  "title": "Variables and Types",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p2-l1",
      "title": "var and :=",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Variables are declared before begin, in a var section, with their type: var age: integer; Pascal wants to know every variable before it's used.",
        "A value is stored with := (say \"becomes\"): age := 13; A single = only compares. Mixing them up gives Error 91: \":=\" expected.",
        "integer holds whole numbers. On the IBM PC, from -32768 to 32767."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Ages;",
          "var",
          "  a, b: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  a := 1983;",
          "  b := 1970;",
          "  writeln('Turbo Pascal came ', a - b, ' years after Pascal.');",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": []
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where are variables declared?",
          "options": [
            "In the var section, before begin",
            "Anywhere in the program",
            "After end.",
            "They don't need to be declared"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Pascal checks every variable before the program runs."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you store 5 in x?",
          "options": [
            "x := 5;",
            "x = 5;",
            "5 -> x;",
            "let x = 5;"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": ":= stores a value. = compares."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Declare two whole-number variables.",
          "code": [
            "var",
            "  a, b: ___;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "integer",
            "int",
            "number",
            "real"
          ],
          "answer": "integer",
          "explanation": "The type for whole numbers is integer."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e4",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Wrong;",
            "var",
            "  n: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  n = 10;",
            "  writeln(n);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "Assignment is := in Pascal: n := 10;"
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Add;",
            "var",
            "  x: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  x := 10;",
            "  x := x + 5;",
            "  writeln(x);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "15",
            "10",
            "5",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "x becomes 10, then x becomes x + 5."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l1-e6",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program stops with Error 3: Unknown identifier. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Count;",
            "var",
            "  n: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  n := 3;",
            "  total := n * 2;",
            "  writeln(total);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 5,
          "explanation": "total was never declared. Add it to the var section: total: integer;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2-l2",
      "title": "div, mod and real",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "For whole numbers, div divides and cuts off the rest: 17 div 5 is 3. mod gives the rest: 17 mod 5 is 2.",
        "The / sign always gives a real number, a number with decimals: 7 / 2 is 3.5. Store it in a variable of type real. Without a format, Turbo Pascal writes reals in scientific notation: 3.5000000000E+00 means 3.5 × 10^0. The next unit shows how to make that prettier.",
        "integer only goes up to 32767. Above that it overflows and turns negative! For bigger whole numbers there's longint (up to about 2 billion)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Numbers;",
          "var",
          "  i: integer;",
          "  big: longint;",
          "  r: real;",
          "begin",
          "  writeln(17 div 5, ' rest ', 17 mod 5);",
          "  r := 7 / 2;",
          "  writeln(r);",
          "  i := 32767;",
          "  i := i + 1;",
          "  writeln(i);",
          "  big := 32767;",
          "  big := big + 1;",
          "  writeln(big);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": []
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 23 div 4?",
          "options": [
            "5",
            "3",
            "5.75",
            "6"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "4 fits 5 times into 23. The rest is dropped."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 23 mod 4?",
          "options": [
            "3",
            "5",
            "0",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "23 = 5 × 4 + 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What type is the result of 10 / 4?",
          "options": [
            "real",
            "integer",
            "longint",
            "char"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "/ always gives a real: 2.5."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e4",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Half;",
            "var",
            "  n: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  n := 9 / 2;",
            "  writeln(n);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "9 / 2 is a real (4.5) and can't go into an integer: Error 26: Type mismatch. Use 9 div 2 for a whole number."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Wrap;",
            "var",
            "  n: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  n := 32767;",
            "  n := n + 1;",
            "  writeln(n);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "-32768",
            "32768",
            "32767",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "integer goes up to 32767. One more wraps around to the smallest value, -32768."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l2-e6",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Get the minutes left over after the full hours.",
          "code": [
            "  minutes := 135 ___ 60;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "mod",
            "div",
            "/",
            "rest"
          ],
          "answer": "mod",
          "explanation": "135 mod 60 is 15: 2 full hours and 15 minutes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p2-l3",
      "title": "char, boolean and string",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "char holds one character: c := 'A'; boolean holds true or false: ok := 5 > 3; string holds text, up to 255 characters: name := 'Niklaus';",
        "length(s) gives the number of characters in a string. Strings can be joined with +.",
        "Pascal is strict about types: a string can't go into an integer, and a real can't go into an integer. That catches many mistakes before the program even runs."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Kinds;",
          "var",
          "  grade: char;",
          "  passed: boolean;",
          "  name: string;",
          "begin",
          "  grade := 'A';",
          "  passed := true;",
          "  name := 'Anders';",
          "  name := name + ' Hejlsberg';",
          "  writeln(name, ' (', length(name), ' characters)');",
          "  writeln('Grade ', grade, ', passed: ', passed);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": []
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which type holds true or false?",
          "options": [
            "boolean",
            "char",
            "bool",
            "integer"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Named after George Boole, who invented the logic of true and false."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does length('Pascal') give?",
          "options": [
            "6",
            "7",
            "5",
            "'Pascal'"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "P-a-s-c-a-l: six characters."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Bool;",
            "var",
            "  big: boolean;",
            "begin",
            "  big := 10 > 3;",
            "  writeln(big);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "TRUE",
            "FALSE",
            "1",
            "yes"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "10 > 3 is true, and Turbo Pascal writes booleans as TRUE or FALSE."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Join the two strings.",
          "code": [
            "  full := first ___ ' ' + last;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "+",
            "&",
            ",",
            "."
          ],
          "answer": "+",
          "explanation": "+ joins strings in Pascal."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Kind;",
            "var",
            "  c: char;",
            "begin",
            "  c := 'AB';",
            "  writeln(c);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "A char holds exactly one character. For 'AB' you need a string."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2-l3-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Who wrote Turbo Pascal?",
          "options": [
            "Anders Hejlsberg",
            "Niklaus Wirth",
            "Bill Gates",
            "Ken Thompson"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Hejlsberg later designed Delphi, C# and TypeScript."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}